.TH CTPOOL_SHUTDOWN_IMMEDIATE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_shutdown_immediate \- immediate shutdown
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_shutdown_immediate(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Cancels every task that is in the queue. It then tells each worker
thread to exit after its current task, if it has one, finishes. It
blocks until every worker thread has exited.

A cancelled task that ctpool_submit(3), ctpool_try_submit(3) or
ctpool_timed_submit(3) queued with an on_complete gets
on_complete(arg, false), and its fn never runs. The calls run on the
thread that calls this function, before it returns, with no lock of
the pool held. From such an on_complete, ctpool_wait(3) and both
shutdown functions on the same pool are no-ops, and ctpool_destroy(3)
on the same pool is a fatal error; see ctpool_submit(3).

The library marks the future of a cancelled task as done and as
cancelled. It wakes every thread that blocks in ctpool_future_get for a
cancelled future. Each of them receives NULL.

A second call does no more shutdown work of its own. That holds both
for a call that follows the first one and for a call that runs at the
same time on another thread. It still blocks until the shutdown that is
already under way stops. Every caller therefore gets the same
guarantee: after the call returns, every worker thread has exited.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed
       handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A task that already runs when you make this call finishes as usual.
Every submit function returns ccol_not_permitted after this call. The
one exception is ctpool_submit_future, which reports a failure only as
a NULL return.

A call to this function while a ctpool_shutdown_drain already runs
escalates that drain. It does not join the drain unchanged. It discards
whatever is still in the queue at that moment, it cancels the futures
of those tasks, and it calls on_complete(arg, false) for each of them
that has one. It behaves exactly as if this call had started
the shutdown. A task that the drain already started still finishes as
usual.

A call to ctpool_shutdown_immediate on pool from inside a task does
nothing at all. That holds when the task runs on one of the worker
threads of pool. A call from the on_complete callback of that task is
the same.
The reason is the one that ctpool_shutdown_drain documents for the same
case: a worker thread cannot join itself. This call neither discards
the queue nor blocks.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
